WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.410 --> 00:00:05.930
- Hi everyone, Sal Khan
here from Khan Academy.

00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:09.000
Welcome to our Daily Homeroom live stream.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:10.850
The whole goal of this is for all of us

00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:14.390
to stay connected during
times of school closures.

00:00:14.390 --> 00:00:15.340
Depending on the day,

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:18.480
this is a time for all of y'all
to ask questions of myself

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:19.990
or other guests or team members

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:21.630
we might have at Khan Academy.

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:24.550
It might be about navigating
school, the school closures,

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:25.740
how to learn a subject

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:27.840
or frankly, whatever
y'all wanna ask about.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:30.370
So feel free if you're
on YouTube or Facebook

00:00:30.370 --> 00:00:32.020
or some other social media channel,

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:34.240
start asking questions
in the message boards.

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:36.740
We have team members who are
looking at those questions

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:38.320
and then surfacing to them.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:39.470
To me on a little doc

00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:41.330
that I have on the screen right over here.

00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:44.060
I also have my colleague Dan,
who's going to be jumping in

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:45.400
and help surface questions.

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:47.030
And then the other interesting things

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:48.270
that y'all might post.

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:49.103
Hey Dan.

00:00:49.103 --> 00:00:52.690
And just as a reminder, Khan
Academy is a not-for-profit.

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:55.800
We have a mission of providing
a free world class education

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:56.720
for anyone, anywhere.

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:59.480
And as you can imagine we
could have never foreseen

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:02.157
this COVID-19 crisis, the school closures,

00:01:02.157 --> 00:01:05.320
but we think it's our duty given

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:07.164
what we've been building
over the last decade

00:01:07.164 --> 00:01:09.800
and the resources we have available

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:11.560
and given our mission
to really try to step up

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.150
as much as we can in this time.

00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:16.010
So we've been trying to
put all of the resources

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:19.010
that we have from Pre-K all
the way through high school

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:20.560
and college together,

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:23.820
so it can be coherent for
parents, teachers and students.

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:25.390
We've been putting together learning plans

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:28.085
and schedules and webinars for all of you

00:01:28.085 --> 00:01:30.586
and we've been doing this live stream.

00:01:30.586 --> 00:01:33.508
We are funded by philanthropic
donations from folks like

00:01:33.508 --> 00:01:37.160
yourself and that becoming
increasingly vocal,

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:40.990
to telling folks that we've
been running at a deficit

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:44.240
even before the crisis hit
and our costs are going up

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:47.070
and there's more that we want
to do because of the crisis,

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:48.600
so that we can accelerate hopefully over

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:50.930
the course of the summer and
this coming back to school.

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:52.200
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.530
please think about
donating to Khan Academy.

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:56.380
I want to give a special shout out to

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:00.602
in the last several weeks,
Bank of America followed

00:02:00.602 --> 00:02:05.602
by AT&amp;T, Google.org and
Novartis and we need more help.

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:07.550
If you're representing a corporation

00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:09.252
please think about donating Khan Academy.

00:02:09.252 --> 00:02:12.944
Donating to Khan Academy in
this time obviously people

00:02:12.944 --> 00:02:14.540
are thinking about the virus,

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:15.480
they thing about mental health,

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:16.750
they thing about the economy,

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:18.620
but how do we keep kids
learning especially

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:21.173
the kids most in need
who might not have access

00:02:21.173 --> 00:02:22.600
to other supports.

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:24.740
I think is, is a, is a
really, really key question

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:27.010
and we want to help where we can.

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:30.614
So with that, I'm happy to
start answering some questions

00:02:30.614 --> 00:02:32.855
that we have here.

00:02:32.855 --> 00:02:35.470
So let's see the first question I have

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:37.550
and I have to say, Susanna
Garcia Domingo says

00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:39.820
you are a regular at asking questions.

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:40.730
I guess good questions.

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:42.640
These are the ones I get surface.

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:46.597
There are no bad questions,
but from YouTube, Susana asks,

00:02:46.597 --> 00:02:51.230
"Hi Sal, what was your
last most memorable meal?

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:55.000
Please describe that experience?"

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:56.960
It's an interesting question.

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:01.960
And my last most memorable
meal, you know what it was,

00:03:03.543 --> 00:03:08.110
it was thanksgiving like
four or five years ago.

00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:13.096
And I had the idea to not eat
to fast before thanksgiving.

00:03:13.096 --> 00:03:16.860
And I have to say when
you, when you fast for,

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:19.430
I think I had fasted for 36 hours,

00:03:19.430 --> 00:03:21.750
kids don't do this at home,
you consult your doctor

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:22.810
and things like that,

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:26.070
but I find every now and then
you know, just a water fast.

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:27.840
So I was drinking water.

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:30.450
It kind of gets your sensory acuity up.

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:34.270
And so going into that
thanksgiving I had a cheesecake

00:03:34.270 --> 00:03:37.030
and it was the best cheesecake I ever had.

00:03:37.030 --> 00:03:38.810
I think it was a good cheesecake

00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:41.753
and I hadn't eaten for
a long period of time.

00:03:41.753 --> 00:03:45.380
So yeah, that was the most memorable meal.

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:46.610
But anyway, no dietary advice.

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:48.751
Please don't go fast
without medical supervision,

00:03:48.751 --> 00:03:50.763
standard disclaimers.

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:53.400
- Sal, so we wanted to do something

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:54.462
a little bit different today.

00:03:54.462 --> 00:03:57.830
We get a lot of amazing
questions on this forum,

00:03:57.830 --> 00:04:00.570
but we also get amazing
posts in social media.

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:02.720
So we thought we'd share a
few with our audience today.

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:06.970
So the first one is from YouTube
comment from Kerpal Singh,

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:09.980
she says, or he says, "I
give a roll of toilet paper

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:12.060
to Salman Khan any day.

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:16.360
Thank you Kerpal, I hope
one day we get a chance

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:18.087
to I guess share toilet paper.

00:04:18.087 --> 00:04:22.760
That's a little, yeah,
actually I've been getting

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:25.240
a lot of toilet paper offers lately,

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:28.500
which I think 'cause it is
like the new gold I guess.

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:30.620
And I just want to let everyone know

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:33.570
that you know, my family, we
consider ourselves fortunate.

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:36.130
We have I think at least
a solid two weeks supply

00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:37.100
of toilet paper at home.

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:41.077
So we're doing good, no
need to worry about that.

00:04:41.077 --> 00:04:45.220
- Great, and then we have
a question on YouTube

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:47.857
from Rashida Idris.

00:04:47.857 --> 00:04:50.817
"How many hours should an
eighth grader study a day?"

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:55.910
How many hours should an
eighth grader study a day?

00:04:55.910 --> 00:05:00.500
So I think there's kind of a
minimum I think in anything,

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:03.280
if you want to get better at
it's good to build a habit

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:04.650
to do a little bit every day.

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:07.250
And so I would say if your eighth grader,

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:08.800
especially in times of school closures,

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:13.800
if you could put in even a
solid focused 30 minutes in each

00:05:13.858 --> 00:05:17.610
of the subjects that you want
to make solid progress in,

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:18.860
you're going to be in great shape

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:20.240
when you go back to school in the fall.

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:23.627
So I would put in at least 30
minutes a day on Khan Academy

00:05:23.627 --> 00:05:25.480
and you could take a break on weekends.

00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:26.710
But Monday through Friday,

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:29.310
try to put at least 30
minutes a day in Khan Academy,

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:32.690
I would do at least 30 minutes on math.

00:05:32.690 --> 00:05:36.140
I would do 30 minutes a day
of reading of some form.

00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:37.920
Find some books that are at your level

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:39.250
that you would really enjoy reading

00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:41.780
and read them for at
least 30 minutes a day.

00:05:41.780 --> 00:05:44.356
And hopefully longer than that
if you really enjoy the book.

00:05:44.356 --> 00:05:48.360
And I would do at least 30
minutes a day of trying to write

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:50.390
and you know, it might
be a little bit longer.

00:05:50.390 --> 00:05:52.050
It could be as simple as journaling.

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:53.640
It could be there's a
lot of stuff going on

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:54.473
in the world today.

00:05:54.473 --> 00:05:56.610
Write opinion pieces, create a blog,

00:05:56.610 --> 00:05:58.270
share it with and family.

00:05:58.270 --> 00:06:00.010
I think if you do those three things,

00:06:00.010 --> 00:06:01.640
you're going to be in decent shape.

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:03.690
And then if you feel like
you have that under control

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:05.911
and you're able to do other
things like go outside

00:06:05.911 --> 00:06:08.840
and run and spend time with your family

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:10.900
and take on some fun projects

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:12.100
that you've always wanted to do,

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:13.420
then you could layer on a little bit more.

00:06:13.420 --> 00:06:16.418
You could maybe do another
30 minutes a day of science

00:06:16.418 --> 00:06:19.300
or and Khan Academy has
a lot of resources there.

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:20.750
We don't have as much as we'd like

00:06:20.750 --> 00:06:21.661
at the middle school level yet.

00:06:21.661 --> 00:06:25.268
But if you are looking to keep going,

00:06:25.268 --> 00:06:27.190
I think you could definitely look at

00:06:27.190 --> 00:06:29.110
some of the beginning
units of our biology,

00:06:29.110 --> 00:06:31.040
high school biology,
high school chemistry,

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:31.960
and even some of the things

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:33.508
on things like Newton's laws and physics.

00:06:33.508 --> 00:06:36.240
You can start getting a leg up.

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:37.873
And so I would say that
that would be a good,

00:06:37.873 --> 00:06:40.890
if you're able to do it consistently,

00:06:40.890 --> 00:06:43.770
that'd be a good amount of
learning on a daily basis.

00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:46.656
Now obviously you could do
more, but I would just say,

00:06:46.656 --> 00:06:49.600
that's great, but just
don't stress yourself out.

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:51.420
This is a time where we should make sure

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:52.920
that we're taking care of ourselves

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:55.664
and having time to run outside and play

00:06:55.664 --> 00:06:57.883
and spend time with family as well.

00:06:59.612 --> 00:07:03.670
So there's a, this is a
good question from YouTube,

00:07:03.670 --> 00:07:07.310
Elliot H who refers to
me as Dumbledore Sal

00:07:07.310 --> 00:07:08.690
and I enjoy that.

00:07:08.690 --> 00:07:10.890
That was part of my
motivation for growing a beard

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:13.042
that hopefully someone would
one day see the resemblance.

00:07:13.042 --> 00:07:17.807
"When is it better to innovate
through business example,

00:07:17.807 --> 00:07:20.447
"Apple, Google or Tesla rather
than through philanthropy,

00:07:20.447 --> 00:07:21.993
"example, Khan Academy, Wikipedia?

00:07:21.993 --> 00:07:24.807
"You judge that a free
world class education

00:07:24.807 --> 00:07:26.527
"would be better achieved
philanthropically.

00:07:26.527 --> 00:07:28.157
"What is the more meaningful innovations

00:07:28.157 --> 00:07:30.110
"happen better through business?"

00:07:30.110 --> 00:07:33.270
That is a very, very
good question, Elliot.

00:07:33.270 --> 00:07:36.780
So the way I think about it, I
do believe generally speaking

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:38.009
in market forces in capitalism.

00:07:38.009 --> 00:07:41.040
And so I think generally free enterprise

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:43.808
and I guess you could
say the profit motive

00:07:43.808 --> 00:07:46.854
when markets function well

00:07:46.854 --> 00:07:49.270
I can create a lot of innovation.

00:07:49.270 --> 00:07:51.430
And some of those things that you know,

00:07:51.430 --> 00:07:54.640
the Googles of the world,
the Teslas, the Apples,

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:57.170
and you can, you can
think of many, many others

00:07:57.170 --> 00:08:00.860
where people deployed capital
and a place where they thought

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:03.530
that they could create something
of value to other people.

00:08:03.530 --> 00:08:05.240
People were willing to pay for that value

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:06.455
that created a return on the capital

00:08:06.455 --> 00:08:09.360
and more money to invest in that effort.

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:10.470
So that's great.

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:13.616
And I actually think rightfully
that is most of our economy.

00:08:13.616 --> 00:08:17.200
But I think there are
segments of our economy

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:20.310
where either the markets for some reason

00:08:20.310 --> 00:08:21.400
aren't working well.

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:23.300
And there's many reasons
why markets sometimes

00:08:23.300 --> 00:08:24.420
don't work well.

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:29.280
There might be externalities
to what the market is capturing

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:31.210
that aren't captured in the price.

00:08:31.210 --> 00:08:32.900
And you know, people think
about some of those things

00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:35.340
in terms of pollution
and things like that.

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:37.717
There's markets where there
isn't transparent information.

00:08:37.717 --> 00:08:42.060
There's markets where
the buyers, the sellers

00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:44.360
and the beneficiaries
are different people.

00:08:44.360 --> 00:08:46.325
So you don't get that same accountability

00:08:46.325 --> 00:08:49.512
that you would have in a
traditional marketplace.

00:08:49.512 --> 00:08:52.061
And there's situations
where the market might work,

00:08:52.061 --> 00:08:55.260
but what it does work,
it leads to outcomes

00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:57.030
aren't consistent with our values.

00:08:57.030 --> 00:09:00.590
And I would say the two areas
where it's not wholesale.

00:09:00.590 --> 00:09:02.080
You know, there's
definitely a lot of space

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:05.790
for profits in education
and in healthcare.

00:09:05.790 --> 00:09:07.940
But those are the two
areas where I would say,

00:09:07.940 --> 00:09:09.939
markets sometimes get a little bit

00:09:09.939 --> 00:09:13.390
kind of fall into that gray area.

00:09:13.390 --> 00:09:16.150
I think we as a society
have a value system

00:09:16.150 --> 00:09:17.920
that if someone wants to learn,

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:19.720
they should be able to learn regardless

00:09:19.720 --> 00:09:20.900
of their parents' income

00:09:20.900 --> 00:09:23.450
or what neighborhood they grow up in.

00:09:23.450 --> 00:09:25.909
And if someone is bleeding

00:09:25.909 --> 00:09:28.450
or dying that we should treat them,

00:09:28.450 --> 00:09:29.770
we should treat them first

00:09:29.770 --> 00:09:31.420
before thinking about
their ability to pay.

00:09:31.420 --> 00:09:34.307
I think those are two universal
values that we all have.

00:09:34.307 --> 00:09:37.766
And sometimes markets aren't
able to make that happen.

00:09:37.766 --> 00:09:40.720
Now somewhat argue that
that's the role of government

00:09:40.720 --> 00:09:42.490
that government can play those roles

00:09:42.490 --> 00:09:45.420
but where I see the philanthropic
sector being valuable

00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:47.940
is that gap of sometimes government

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:50.500
is a little bit slow
to move into something

00:09:50.500 --> 00:09:53.770
they may be not, can't innovate as fast.

00:09:53.770 --> 00:09:57.210
And then on the for profit
side, they can innovate fast,

00:09:57.210 --> 00:09:58.750
but sometimes the motivations

00:09:58.750 --> 00:10:00.610
aren't completely aligned
with the value system.

00:10:00.610 --> 00:10:03.858
And so that's where I think
a philanthropic of sector

00:10:03.858 --> 00:10:05.210
is really valuable.

00:10:05.210 --> 00:10:07.120
And you know, Khan Academy in theory

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:08.640
could have been a for profit company.

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:09.820
In fact, in the early days,

00:10:09.820 --> 00:10:11.340
some of the first folks I talked to

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:12.507
were investors who was like,

00:10:12.507 --> 00:10:14.357
"Hey, we can make this a real thing."

00:10:15.476 --> 00:10:19.140
The conversations around
how do you monetize it?

00:10:19.140 --> 00:10:20.950
Oh, we could give this stuff for free.

00:10:20.950 --> 00:10:23.460
But then we would have
to charge for that stuff.

00:10:23.460 --> 00:10:25.700
That felt a little bit
inconsistent with at least

00:10:25.700 --> 00:10:29.077
what I was hoping Khan
Academy could become.

00:10:29.077 --> 00:10:31.830
And when I looked through the world

00:10:31.830 --> 00:10:33.700
and history of institutions

00:10:33.700 --> 00:10:37.490
that had been able to serve many millions

00:10:37.490 --> 00:10:39.270
or hundreds of millions
or billions of people

00:10:39.270 --> 00:10:41.930
and have been able to
do it over generations.

00:10:41.930 --> 00:10:44.300
So not just that first 20 or 30 years

00:10:44.300 --> 00:10:45.790
where you might have that founding team

00:10:45.790 --> 00:10:47.130
that wants to operate in a certain way,

00:10:47.130 --> 00:10:49.280
but over many, many, many generations.

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:51.760
It's consistently, it's nonprofits.

00:10:51.760 --> 00:10:53.191
I think even some of the great for-profits

00:10:53.191 --> 00:10:55.840
that we know today, history would tell us

00:10:55.840 --> 00:10:57.580
that in 30 or 40 or 50 years,

00:10:57.580 --> 00:10:59.541
they'll probably be very,
very different organizations

00:10:59.541 --> 00:11:01.200
than they are right now.

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:04.340
While many of the not-for-profits
have a little bit more

00:11:04.340 --> 00:11:08.360
of a strong narrative
over multiple generations,

00:11:08.360 --> 00:11:09.200
and it was delusional

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:11.057
when someone operating
out of a walk in closet.

00:11:11.057 --> 00:11:14.034
but I do hope increasingly
and I'm more open about it,

00:11:14.034 --> 00:11:16.869
that Khan Academy goes well beyond me.

00:11:16.869 --> 00:11:20.892
And that in a hundred years,
folks around the planet

00:11:20.892 --> 00:11:24.838
are like, of course access to
a free world class education

00:11:24.838 --> 00:11:26.430
for anyone anywhere is there.

00:11:26.430 --> 00:11:28.210
And who knows what kind of
Academy looks like then,

00:11:28.210 --> 00:11:31.450
it might be some type of
virtual reality experience.

00:11:31.450 --> 00:11:34.360
It might be a pill you
take all of a sudden,

00:11:34.360 --> 00:11:39.360
you know, a lot of things who
knows that was the thinking.

00:11:39.575 --> 00:11:43.770
- Sal, we have a question
on YouTube from a teacher,

00:11:43.770 --> 00:11:45.727
Schendel red queen of Wonderland asked,

00:11:45.727 --> 00:11:48.797
"Mr. Khan, do you have
any advice for leadership?

00:11:48.797 --> 00:11:50.857
"I've been trying to host
Zoom meetings with my classes

00:11:50.857 --> 00:11:52.997
"and I find it really difficult to do so."

00:11:55.110 --> 00:11:58.410
- So Schendel I'm assuming when you say

00:11:58.410 --> 00:12:00.325
you've been trying to
advice for leadership

00:12:00.325 --> 00:12:03.330
with your classes, I'm
assuming you're talking about

00:12:03.330 --> 00:12:05.760
like as kind of teaching or
teaching virtually, although,

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:09.410
I could give my two cents on
leadership generally although

00:12:09.410 --> 00:12:11.440
I'm sure many of the
Khan Academy team members

00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:13.066
might roll their eyes.

00:12:13.066 --> 00:12:15.110
(laughs)

00:12:15.110 --> 00:12:16.920
I'm sure they have a better sense of

00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:18.349
how effective my leadership is or not.

00:12:18.349 --> 00:12:20.930
But you know, if you're
teaching virtually,

00:12:20.930 --> 00:12:22.650
and I have been working
with several teachers here

00:12:22.650 --> 00:12:24.692
and I've been doing a
few myself volunteering

00:12:24.692 --> 00:12:26.819
trying to teach virtually,

00:12:26.819 --> 00:12:30.360
it actually takes me back to 2004

00:12:30.360 --> 00:12:32.090
when I was tutoring my cousins

00:12:32.090 --> 00:12:33.610
and we didn't have Zoom back then,

00:12:33.610 --> 00:12:35.220
we didn't have Google Hangouts back then,

00:12:35.220 --> 00:12:36.600
but I was trying to use the phone

00:12:36.600 --> 00:12:39.720
and we had some tablet software

00:12:39.720 --> 00:12:41.430
that we could see each other's writing.

00:12:41.430 --> 00:12:44.830
And what I found is
the more virtual it is,

00:12:44.830 --> 00:12:47.013
the more that you have to
really drive interaction.

00:12:47.013 --> 00:12:50.782
So I've run a couple of sessions
along with some teachers

00:12:50.782 --> 00:12:53.574
in a school district and
out here in California,

00:12:53.574 --> 00:12:55.954
and what we're all trying to do is,

00:12:55.954 --> 00:12:58.700
you know, put up a problem,
just an example problem,

00:12:58.700 --> 00:13:00.880
it doesn't have to be a
super structured lesson

00:13:00.880 --> 00:13:02.230
but you put up an example problem

00:13:02.230 --> 00:13:03.410
could just be on a white board.

00:13:03.410 --> 00:13:05.700
If you have a digital tablet,
you can share your screen

00:13:05.700 --> 00:13:06.533
and then you say,

00:13:06.533 --> 00:13:09.038
"Okay, who has some
ideas on the first step?"

00:13:09.038 --> 00:13:11.633
And so the more, and then
even cold call people like,

00:13:11.633 --> 00:13:15.530
"Hey Michael, what do you
think is the next step?"

00:13:15.530 --> 00:13:16.993
And so I think the more that you can get

00:13:16.993 --> 00:13:20.890
that inter that exchange the better

00:13:20.890 --> 00:13:22.530
and the more engaging it's going to be,

00:13:22.530 --> 00:13:25.014
especially if you're doing
it on a modality like Zoom.

00:13:25.014 --> 00:13:28.638
What what I say is, if
it's just me lecturing

00:13:28.638 --> 00:13:31.210
the whole time, that's so
different than a video.

00:13:31.210 --> 00:13:32.880
So there's no reason to be on Zoom.

00:13:32.880 --> 00:13:34.170
You can just record that as a video

00:13:34.170 --> 00:13:35.670
and make it a more available.

00:13:35.670 --> 00:13:37.560
So that's my general advice.

00:13:37.560 --> 00:13:39.763
Leadership advice broadly,

00:13:41.060 --> 00:13:43.450
I am on a journey myself

00:13:43.450 --> 00:13:47.618
to learn a lot there, but
I'm a big believer that

00:13:47.618 --> 00:13:50.600
the more that you can be authentic,

00:13:50.600 --> 00:13:54.256
be your true self the more that you can,

00:13:54.256 --> 00:13:57.378
I think sometimes people say,

00:13:57.378 --> 00:14:00.757
"Oh, I have to be careful
what I need to say,

00:14:00.757 --> 00:14:02.577
"because it might come off the wrong way.

00:14:02.577 --> 00:14:04.340
"And so therefore I can't be authentic."

00:14:04.340 --> 00:14:07.120
But what I found is you gotta
be careful what you think

00:14:07.120 --> 00:14:09.850
and if you're thinking good thoughts,

00:14:09.850 --> 00:14:12.890
if you're thinking
thoughts that would pass,

00:14:12.890 --> 00:14:14.520
let's call it the New York Times test,

00:14:14.520 --> 00:14:15.930
which is if your thoughts were published

00:14:15.930 --> 00:14:17.260
in the New York Times,

00:14:17.260 --> 00:14:19.296
would it be a good thing or bad thing?

00:14:19.296 --> 00:14:21.910
If you can get your mind
to the point of like,

00:14:21.910 --> 00:14:23.720
you're genuinely thinking good thoughts,

00:14:23.720 --> 00:14:25.871
you're thinking of what
is best for others.

00:14:25.871 --> 00:14:28.020
We all have thoughts every now and then

00:14:28.020 --> 00:14:29.936
that we're not proud of.

00:14:29.936 --> 00:14:33.460
I've had my sheriff but isn't,
you have a little process.

00:14:33.460 --> 00:14:35.230
We were like, that's not a good thought.

00:14:35.230 --> 00:14:36.990
That's yeah, that was a petty thought

00:14:36.990 --> 00:14:40.060
or that was a thought, that
thought was driven by your ego

00:14:40.060 --> 00:14:42.450
and not what's best for other folks.

00:14:42.450 --> 00:14:45.280
If you can get good at that,
then I think that liberates you

00:14:45.280 --> 00:14:47.160
to be very authentic with your leadership

00:14:47.160 --> 00:14:50.390
because you are thinking things
that you're not ashamed of.

00:14:50.390 --> 00:14:53.240
You know, someone I met when they said,

00:14:53.240 --> 00:14:55.470
the best way to look like
you care about people

00:14:55.470 --> 00:14:57.360
is to actually care about people.

00:14:57.360 --> 00:15:00.480
So that's a little bit of a
little bit of a hack in life.

00:15:00.480 --> 00:15:03.930
So I would say yeah, I
would say there's that.

00:15:03.930 --> 00:15:06.300
And you know, always being
able to balance that tension

00:15:06.300 --> 00:15:09.316
between what your heart tells you is right

00:15:09.316 --> 00:15:14.316
and what your head tells
you is a reasonable approach

00:15:14.408 --> 00:15:16.730
to being able to get there.

00:15:16.730 --> 00:15:19.510
You know, throughout the last 10, 12 years

00:15:19.510 --> 00:15:21.010
of this Khan Academy journey,

00:15:21.010 --> 00:15:23.730
we've always had these debates
inside of the organization

00:15:23.730 --> 00:15:25.820
of like, okay, we know that
there's that mountain someplace

00:15:25.820 --> 00:15:27.290
that we want to get to the top of,

00:15:27.290 --> 00:15:29.510
that's a world in which literally billions

00:15:29.510 --> 00:15:31.753
of people have access to a
free world class education.

00:15:31.753 --> 00:15:34.450
Now we, there's a fog.

00:15:34.450 --> 00:15:35.888
We don't know exactly how to get there

00:15:35.888 --> 00:15:38.590
and we'll just try to follow the gradient

00:15:38.590 --> 00:15:40.090
and work our way through that fog

00:15:40.090 --> 00:15:40.990
to the top of that mountain.

00:15:40.990 --> 00:15:43.620
And we're gonna we're
going to make mistakes,

00:15:43.620 --> 00:15:45.950
but you really have to do is say, "okay,

00:15:45.950 --> 00:15:48.180
sometimes your heart might make you want

00:15:48.180 --> 00:15:49.430
to go directly for that mountain,

00:15:49.430 --> 00:15:52.260
but there might be a pit between,

00:15:52.260 --> 00:15:53.350
so you're not gonna be
able to go to the mountain.

00:15:53.350 --> 00:15:55.760
So your head has to say, no,
we got to go a hundred meters

00:15:55.760 --> 00:15:57.389
to the right and cross
that bridge over there.

00:15:57.389 --> 00:15:59.571
The other thing I would say is,

00:15:59.571 --> 00:16:02.404
it's a lot like a chess game.

00:16:02.404 --> 00:16:05.420
A good chess player doesn't know exactly

00:16:05.420 --> 00:16:07.350
how they're going to
win the game in the end,

00:16:07.350 --> 00:16:09.600
but they are definitely
setting up their board

00:16:09.600 --> 00:16:11.727
so that they have as much
optionality as possible

00:16:11.727 --> 00:16:14.540
and they have as much
protection as possible.

00:16:14.540 --> 00:16:16.040
So they're getting their pieces out there,

00:16:16.040 --> 00:16:17.900
opening it up, they're trying to control

00:16:17.900 --> 00:16:18.940
the center of the board.

00:16:18.940 --> 00:16:21.380
And so I'd say leadership
or strategy generally,

00:16:21.380 --> 00:16:22.380
that's a good way to think about it.

00:16:22.380 --> 00:16:24.226
You're never going to have
the whole thing figured out,

00:16:24.226 --> 00:16:26.700
but never let the perfect
be the enemy of the good.

00:16:26.700 --> 00:16:28.260
Anyway, I'll get off my soap box.

00:16:28.260 --> 00:16:30.260
I don't know if that's useful at all.

