00:19:00.278 --> 00:19:03.453
And then physics, once you've learned

00:19:03.453 --> 00:19:06.770
the basics of a little bit,

00:19:06.770 --> 00:19:08.480
and this is probably
beyond where you are now,

00:19:08.480 --> 00:19:09.961
if you're in middle
school are low, who knows?

00:19:09.961 --> 00:19:11.806
But once you get the basics of quadratics

00:19:11.806 --> 00:19:13.970
and some of the basics of trigonometry,

00:19:13.970 --> 00:19:15.630
you can also think
about going into physics

00:19:15.630 --> 00:19:19.020
and other humanity side,
we have a lot of content

00:19:19.020 --> 00:19:20.594
on civics and American
history and world history.

00:19:20.594 --> 00:19:23.400
I think you're, especially
if you're looking

00:19:23.400 --> 00:19:24.430
to kind of push yourself,

00:19:24.430 --> 00:19:26.649
you're probably already
ready for that material,

00:19:26.649 --> 00:19:29.170
and our official SAT practice

00:19:29.170 --> 00:19:30.530
actually could be interesting as well.

00:19:30.530 --> 00:19:32.240
That's math, reading and writing.

00:19:32.240 --> 00:19:34.100
And what a lot of folks don't realize

00:19:34.100 --> 00:19:35.500
people normally take the SAT

00:19:35.500 --> 00:19:36.976
and their junior or senior
years of high school,

00:19:36.976 --> 00:19:39.230
but a lot of that content is essentially

00:19:39.230 --> 00:19:40.250
at a middle school level.

00:19:40.250 --> 00:19:41.834
They actually, the highest math on the SAT

00:19:41.834 --> 00:19:44.631
is some of the more basic
concepts from algebra two,

00:19:44.631 --> 00:19:47.460
which a lot of folks
cover in, in 10th grade.

00:19:47.460 --> 00:19:50.230
But most of the math on the SAT is stuff

00:19:50.230 --> 00:19:52.480
that you will see in
eighth or ninth grade.

00:19:52.480 --> 00:19:54.020
And if you're trying to get ahead,

00:19:54.020 --> 00:19:55.620
you're probably gonna see
that a little bit earlier.

00:19:55.620 --> 00:19:58.231
So that'd be by my best advice there.

